=== L2 Targeting Audiences: Marketing, Trailers and Viral Campaigns ===
id: d0b29e3d-10cd-49b5-8a79-38a40c51d498
slug: targeting-audiences-marketing-trailers-and-viral-campaigns
status: live tier:both updated:2026-08-19T11:13:10.8945+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 23

--- DESCRIPTION ---
How organisations profile target audiences and the marketing techniques used to reach them.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">Who Is the Product For?</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">Before a media organisation invests in production, it must decide who the product is intended to reach. A <dfn class="term" data-def="The specific group of people a media product is primarily designed to attract and address.">target audience</dfn> is the specific group at whom a product is aimed. The target audience shapes every decision &mdash; the storylines chosen, the celebrities cast, the platforms used, the price charged. Without a clear target audience, marketing spending is wasted.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n3">A target audience is often divided into a <dfn class="term" data-def="The main intended audience a product is produced for.">primary audience</dfn> and a <dfn class="term" data-def="An additional audience a product may reach beyond its primary target.">secondary audience</dfn>. A magazine pitched at women aged 25&ndash;35 may also attract younger or older readers who share its interests &mdash; those are the secondary audience. Producers do not necessarily design for them, but they welcome them.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n4">Mass and Niche Audiences</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n5">Media products can target either a <dfn class="term" data-def="A very large, broad audience that a media product tries to appeal to regardless of age, gender or background.">mass audience</dfn> or a <dfn class="term" data-def="A small, specialised audience with a specific, shared interest or characteristic.">niche audience</dfn>. A mainstream national daily newspaper tries to reach the widest possible readership; a specialist cycling magazine is produced for a small group with a defined passion. The economics of the two are quite different: mass-audience products attract large advertising revenues; niche products often charge subscription premiums because they deliver advertisers a highly targeted, engaged readership that is harder to reach through mainstream channels.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n6" data-revision-tip="Cover this and explain in one sentence the economic reason why niche media products can still be profitable despite having far fewer readers or viewers than mass-audience products.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Niche audiences are commercially valuable because they deliver advertisers a concentrated, highly engaged group. Advertisers often pay a premium for access to a defined niche rather than broadcasting to a mass audience where most members are not potential customers.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n7">Assumptions Organisations Make About Audiences</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n8">Media producers rarely have direct contact with their audience. Instead they build an <dfn class="term" data-def="A constructed set of assumptions about the typical member of a target audience, including their interests, values, habits and spending power.">audience profile</dfn> &mdash; a set of assumptions about who is likely to be watching, reading, listening or playing. These assumptions are built from research: surveys, focus groups, ratings data and increasingly from behavioural data collected online. Assumptions can also be shaped by stereotypes of certain demographic groups, which is one reason why audience profiles sometimes reinforce existing inequalities.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n9">Genre conventions play a part here too. A horror film producer makes assumptions about who enjoys that genre based on prior audience data &mdash; and those assumptions then shape the marketing approach, the certificate sought from the classifier, and the platforms where the product is promoted.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Trailers, Teasers and Tasters</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n10">The trailer is one of the oldest audience-targeting tools in media. A <dfn class="term" data-def="A short promotional film or broadcast that previews a media product to attract an audience.">trailer</dfn> condenses the appeal of a product into 60&ndash;180 seconds, targeting the assumed audience through music, cast, pace, and the moments selected. A teaser is shorter &mdash; often only 30 seconds &mdash; and deliberately withholds more information in order to build anticipation and conversation. A taster, more common in broadcasting, offers a brief clip or preview episode to give audiences a genuine sense of the content without fully revealing it.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n11">Each type of promotional material makes choices about what to emphasise and what to conceal. A streaming-era teen drama might release a teaser that focuses on the emotional relationships between characters rather than the plot twist at the series&rsquo; end, because the target audience&rsquo;s interest has been identified as character-driven. Those choices reflect assumptions about the audience.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n12">Viral and Guerrilla Marketing</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n13"><dfn class="term" data-def="A marketing strategy designed to encourage audiences to share content voluntarily, spreading awareness organically rather than through paid advertising.">Viral marketing</dfn> attempts to generate audience momentum without (or alongside) conventional paid advertising. The goal is for audiences to share, discuss and amplify the content themselves &mdash; making them unpaid distributors of the marketing message. An effective viral campaign exploits curiosity, humour, emotion or social identity to motivate sharing.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n14"><dfn class="term" data-def="An unconventional, low-cost marketing approach that creates surprising or interactive encounters with an audience in unexpected public or digital spaces.">Guerrilla marketing</dfn> uses surprise, spectacle or novelty to create shareable moments. A product might appear overnight on city hoardings without explanation, leaving audiences curious; an unusual public stunt or pop-up installation might generate news coverage and social media posts without any paid placement. The logic is that earned media &mdash; coverage and discussion that isn&rsquo;t bought &mdash; carries more credibility than advertising because it appears organic.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n15" data-revision-tip="Close this and explain the difference between viral marketing and guerrilla marketing in two sentences, including one example context for each.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Viral marketing relies on audiences sharing content voluntarily across digital platforms. Guerrilla marketing creates surprise encounters in unexpected physical or digital spaces to generate word-of-mouth and news coverage. Both aim to reach audiences without relying solely on paid advertising.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n16">Social Media Activations and ARGs</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n17">Contemporary marketing campaigns extend across multiple platforms. Social media activations coordinate posts, countdowns, and interactive challenges across platforms to build anticipation and engage target audiences directly. An <dfn class="term" data-def="An interactive networked narrative that uses the real world as a platform for storytelling, blurring the boundary between game and reality to engage a target audience.">alternate reality game</dfn> (ARG) goes further: clues are planted across websites, social platforms and physical spaces, inviting audiences to become investigators who unravel a story. ARGs work especially well for products targeting digitally engaged younger audiences who enjoy participatory, puzzle-based experiences.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Owned, Earned and Paid Media</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n18">Marketing professionals think in three categories. <strong>Paid media</strong> is advertising you buy: sponsored posts, television commercials, banner ads. <strong>Owned media</strong> is content you control directly: your official social media accounts, website, and branded YouTube channel. <strong>Earned media</strong> is coverage and discussion generated by others: a journalist writing about your campaign, a fan community speculating about your product, or viewers sharing your trailer. A modern marketing campaign tries to convert paid media investment into earned media reach, which carries credibility because it is not perceived as advertising.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n20">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n21">Media organisations construct detailed audience profiles based on research and assumptions about their target audiences, which shape every production and marketing decision.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n22">Trailers, teasers and tasters are traditional targeting tools; viral marketing, guerrilla marketing and social media activations are digital-era techniques that encourage audiences to become unpaid distributors of marketing messages.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n23">The paid &mdash; owned &mdash; earned media framework explains how organisations try to convert advertising spend into organic reach and credibility.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n19">When a question asks you to explain targeting or marketing techniques, name the specific technique, explain how it works, and then link it to the assumptions the producer is making about their target audience. The examiner wants to see that you understand the relationship between audience profiling and the marketing choices made &mdash; not just a description of what a trailer is.</p>

--- PRACTICE_QUESTIONS ---
