=== L3 Categorising and Measuring Audiences ===
id: 2834bd84-7c2c-4cb2-bb5e-4d04968e36e9
slug: categorising-and-measuring-audiences
status: live tier:both updated:2026-08-19T11:13:08.946793+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 29

--- DESCRIPTION ---
How audiences are segmented by demographic, psychographic and geographic variables, and how BARB and RAJAR measure them.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">Why Categorise Audiences?</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">Media organisations cannot sell advertising space or justify production budgets simply by saying &ldquo;lots of people watch this.&rdquo; Advertisers want to know <em>which</em> people &mdash; their age, income, values and habits. This is why the media industry invests heavily in audience research and categorisation. Three main types of segmentation are used: <dfn class="term" data-def="Dividing audiences according to measurable social characteristics such as age, gender, ethnicity, education and income.">demographic segmentation</dfn>, <dfn class="term" data-def="Dividing audiences according to their attitudes, values, interests and lifestyles.">psychographic segmentation</dfn>, and <dfn class="term" data-def="Dividing audiences according to where they live, from country and region down to postcode.">geographic segmentation</dfn>.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n3">The NRS Social Grade System</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n4">The most widely used demographic tool in UK media is the <dfn class="term" data-def="A UK audience classification system dividing the population into six grades (A, B, C1, C2, D, E) based on the occupation of the chief income earner in the household.">NRS social grade</dfn> system, developed by the National Readership Survey and also used by JICNARS. The six grades are:</p>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n5"><strong>A</strong> &mdash; higher managerial, administrative or professional occupations (e.g. senior managers, doctors, solicitors)</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n6"><strong>B</strong> &mdash; intermediate managerial, administrative or professional occupations (e.g. teachers, nurses, junior managers)</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n7"><strong>C1</strong> &mdash; supervisory, clerical and junior managerial occupations (e.g. office supervisors, sales staff)</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n8"><strong>C2</strong> &mdash; skilled manual workers (e.g. electricians, plumbers)</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n9"><strong>D</strong> &mdash; semi-skilled and unskilled manual workers</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n10"><strong>E</strong> &mdash; those at the lowest levels of subsistence, including long-term unemployed and state pension recipients</li>
</ul>
<p data-narration-id="n11">Grade A and B individuals are often grouped as &ldquo;ABC1&rdquo; to describe an affluent, educated demographic; C2DE describes a working-class, lower-income grouping. These labels appear constantly in media planning documents and advertising rate cards.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n12" data-revision-tip="Cover this and name all six NRS grades from A to E, writing one occupational example for each.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The NRS social grades run from A (higher managerial and professional) to E (those at the lowest subsistence level). Grade A is not simply &ldquo;wealthy&rdquo; &mdash; it is defined by occupation type, not income level alone. This is the UK&rsquo;s standard demographic segmentation tool for media audiences.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n13">Psychographic Segmentation</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n14">Demographic data tells you who audiences are; psychographic data tells you how they think and what they value. Several frameworks exist. Young and Rubicam&rsquo;s <dfn class="term" data-def="Young and Rubicam's psychographic model dividing consumers into four main categories based on values, motivations and attitudes: Mainstreamers, Aspirers, Succeeders and Reformers (plus cross-category types).">4Cs model</dfn> divides consumers by motivation. In the UK, the Values, Attitudes and Lifestyles (VALS) framework and variations on it have been applied to audience planning. Psychographic profiling allows an advertiser to target not just who a person is, but why they might want a product &mdash; their aspirations, anxieties and identity markers.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n15">A streaming service launching a new nature documentary, for example, might target a psychographic profile defined by curiosity, environmental concern and cultural aspiration rather than simply by age or income bracket. The content and marketing would then be calibrated to speak to those values.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Behavioural and First-Party Data</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n16">Digital platforms have moved beyond traditional segmentation by collecting <dfn class="term" data-def="Data collected directly by an organisation about its own users, based on their actual behaviour on that platform.">first-party data</dfn> &mdash; detailed records of what individual users actually watch, click, pause and search for. This behavioural data is far more granular than any demographic or psychographic category. A streaming platform knows not just that a user is a 28-year-old urban professional, but that they always finish crime dramas on weekday evenings, abandon romantic comedies after 20 minutes, and start a new series every 12 days. Those behavioural signals drive recommendation algorithms and inform commissioning decisions.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n17">BARB: Measuring Television Audiences</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n18">The most authoritative television audience measurement in the UK is conducted by <dfn class="term" data-def="The Broadcasters&rsquo; Audience Research Board, the organisation responsible for measuring television viewing figures in the UK.">BARB</dfn> (the Broadcasters&rsquo; Audience Research Board). BARB recruits a panel of households across the UK whose viewing behaviour is monitored electronically via meters on television sets and devices. The panel is designed to be statistically representative of the UK population. Data is collected daily and reported the following day as overnight ratings, with consolidated figures (including catch-up viewing) reported later.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n19">The figures BARB produces &mdash; <dfn class="term" data-def="The total number of people who watch at least some of a broadcast, expressed as an absolute number or percentage of the population.">reach</dfn> and <dfn class="term" data-def="The percentage of people watching television at a given time who are watching a particular channel.">share</dfn> &mdash; are the currency of television commissioning and advertising sales. A drama that achieves a high overnight rating justifies its production cost; a low share on a Friday night might indicate poor scheduling rather than poor content.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n20" data-revision-tip="Close this and write down: what BARB stands for, what it measures, and the difference between reach and share.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>BARB (Broadcasters&rsquo; Audience Research Board) measures UK television viewing using a representative household panel. It reports two key figures: reach (total viewers of a broadcast) and share (the percentage of the television audience watching a particular channel at a given time).</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n21">RAJAR, Pamco and Nielsen</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n22"><dfn class="term" data-def="Radio Joint Audience Research, the organisation that measures radio listening audiences in the UK.">RAJAR</dfn> (Radio Joint Audience Research) performs the equivalent function for radio. Participants complete listening diaries over a set period recording what they listened to, when, and for how long. RAJAR data informs advertising rates and scheduling decisions across BBC and commercial radio.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n23">Pamco (previously known as NRS) measures readership and reach for magazines and newspapers, while <dfn class="term" data-def="An international audience measurement company providing data on digital and online audience behaviour.">Nielsen</dfn> provides global audience data including digital and streaming metrics. Together these bodies provide the quantitative evidence that underpins the entire commercial media economy.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Quantitative vs Qualitative Research</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n24">BARB and RAJAR produce <dfn class="term" data-def="Research that measures audiences in numbers, such as viewing figures, reach and share percentages.">quantitative research</dfn> &mdash; numbers and percentages that allow broadcasters to compare performance and set advertising rates. But numbers alone do not explain why audiences watch, what they enjoy, or how they feel. <dfn class="term" data-def="Research that explores audience attitudes, experiences and motivations through methods such as focus groups and interviews.">Qualitative research</dfn> methods such as focus groups and in-depth interviews give richer insight. A film studio might commission quantitative research to discover how many 16&ndash;24-year-olds saw a trailer, and qualitative research to discover whether they found it exciting or confusing.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n26">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n27">Audiences are categorised by demographic (who they are), psychographic (what they value) and geographic (where they live) segmentation; the NRS social grades A&ndash;E are the UK&rsquo;s standard demographic tool, defined by occupation not wealth.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n28">BARB measures UK television audiences using a representative household panel; RAJAR measures radio listening; Pamco covers print; Nielsen covers digital &mdash; collectively they provide the quantitative data underpinning commercial media.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n29">Quantitative research provides viewing figures and reach; qualitative research &mdash; through focus groups and interviews &mdash; explains audience motivation and response.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n25">When discussing audience categorisation, be precise about which segmentation method you are using &mdash; demographic, psychographic or geographic. A common mistake is using &ldquo;demographic&rdquo; as a catch-all for all forms of categorisation. In longer answers, show you understand why a particular method was chosen: a luxury car advertiser and a fast-food brand will use the same NRS system differently because they are targeting different social grades for different reasons.</p>

--- PRACTICE_QUESTIONS ---
