=== L5 Media Practices, Identity and Fandom ===
id: 2688da63-7315-4ab5-9d1d-e23fb63fe25f
slug: media-practices-identity-and-fandom
status: live tier:both updated:2026-08-19T11:13:09.582657+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 23

--- DESCRIPTION ---
How everyday media use builds identity, and the role of fandom and participatory culture in modern media life.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">Media as Part of Daily Life</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">Media use is not something that happens in a separate compartment of life &mdash; it is woven into everyday routines. People check their phones before getting out of bed, listen to music while commuting, stream video during meals, and scroll social feeds late at night. These <dfn class="term" data-def="The habitual and purposeful ways in which people engage with media products as part of their everyday lives.">media practices</dfn> are not random: they connect to who people are, who they want to be, and how they relate to others.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n3">Media and Identity: Gauntlett&rsquo;s Framework</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n4">David Gauntlett&rsquo;s research &mdash; most fully developed in <em>Media, Gender and Identity</em> &mdash; argues that media use is a key resource through which people actively construct their identity. Rather than simply being told who they are by the media, audiences use media content to explore and develop a sense of self. Gauntlett distinguishes between the <dfn class="term" data-def="The identity a person currently has — who they are as they experience themselves now.">actual self</dfn> and the <dfn class="term" data-def="The identity a person aspires to have — who they would like to become.">desired self</dfn>. Media products can serve both: a viewer might identify with characters who reflect their current identity, or they might be drawn to representations of the person they hope to become.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n5">Gauntlett&rsquo;s approach contrasts sharply with the passive-audience or hypodermic models. Where earlier effects researchers worried that audiences absorb media values without reflection, Gauntlett argued that the audience is always an active reader, picking and choosing what resonates. Identity construction is ongoing and selective, not a one-time injection.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n6" data-revision-tip="Cover this and explain Gauntlett&rsquo;s distinction between actual self and desired self in two sentences, linking it to how a specific type of media product might serve each.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>David Gauntlett argues that audiences use media as a resource for identity construction, drawing on both their actual self (who they currently are) and their desired self (who they want to become). This makes media use an active, ongoing process of self-definition, not passive absorption.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n7">Participatory Culture and Henry Jenkins</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n8">Henry Jenkins&rsquo; concept of <dfn class="term" data-def="Henry Jenkins&rsquo; term for a culture in which audiences are not just consumers but active participants who create, share and contribute to media content.">participatory culture</dfn> describes a media environment in which the boundary between producer and consumer has blurred. In Jenkins&rsquo; account, audiences &mdash; especially fans &mdash; are not content to passively consume media products. They discuss, critique, remix, reimagine and produce their own content in response. This was central to his 1992 book <em>Textual Poachers</em>, where he argued that fans actively &ldquo;poach&rdquo; texts, taking elements of existing media products and remaking them according to their own interests and desires.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n9">Jenkins did not frame this as a modern digital phenomenon &mdash; fan creativity preceded the internet, through fanzines, fan letters and club meetings. But digital networks have dramatically accelerated the scale and visibility of participatory culture, making fan production (fan fiction, fan videos, commentary channels, cosplay documentation) accessible to millions.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Fandom and Stan Culture</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n10"><dfn class="term" data-def="An organised community of enthusiastic followers of a media product, genre, performer or media figure.">Fandom</dfn> describes both the state of being a devoted fan and the community that forms around shared devotion to a media product. Fan communities provide a sense of belonging, shared language and social identity. They mobilise around new releases, create rankings, theorise plot developments, and generate enormous amounts of secondary content. In the digital era, fan communities organise rapidly on social platforms, coordinating activities such as streaming events to boost chart positions or directing collective energy towards media organisations.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n11">Contemporary &ldquo;stan culture&rdquo; &mdash; from the Eminem song &ldquo;Stan&rdquo; (2000), now used generically &mdash; refers to highly dedicated, sometimes intensely devoted fan behaviour. Stan culture shows both the creative and social power of participatory fandom and some of its less positive dynamics, including harassment of critics, extreme parasocial attachment, and fan communities turning on media creators when their decisions disappoint.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n12" data-revision-tip="Close this and name Jenkins&rsquo; 1992 book, summarise his core argument about fans in one sentence, and explain what &lsquo;poaching&rsquo; means in this context.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Henry Jenkins&rsquo; <em>Textual Poachers</em> (1992) argued that fans are active cultural participants who &ldquo;poach&rdquo; texts &mdash; taking existing media products and remaking or reimagining them according to their own interests. This is the foundation of participatory culture theory.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n13">Parasocial Relationships and the Watercooler Effect</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n14">One of the most striking features of modern media use is the development of <dfn class="term" data-def="A one-sided relationship in which an audience member feels a sense of intimacy, familiarity and emotional connection with a media personality who does not know they exist.">parasocial relationships</dfn>. Audiences develop genuine feelings of familiarity, affection and even grief towards performers, influencers and fictional characters who will never know them. These relationships fulfil real social and psychological needs &mdash; particularly the integration and social interaction need identified in Blumler and Katz&rsquo;s Uses and Gratifications theory.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n15">Media use also generates the <dfn class="term" data-def="The social phenomenon where a shared media experience (such as a popular drama episode) generates conversation and debate among people who watched it.">watercooler effect</dfn>: a shared cultural text becomes a topic of conversation, connecting people who otherwise have little in common. A major sporting final, a dramatic episode of a serialised drama, or a divisive viral video all create shared cultural reference points that serve the social function of bringing people into connection with each other.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n16">The Social and Cultural Significance of Media Practices</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n17">Media practices have significance beyond entertainment. The media contributes to shared cultural identity by providing common stories, shared news, and collective experiences. It fulfils individual needs for information, companionship and self-expression &mdash; particularly important for people who are isolated or who belong to communities not well represented in mainstream media. The ability to access online communities through social platforms and streaming has given niche audiences the opportunity to find each other and build shared culture around media products that mainstream broadcasters would never have commissioned.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Second Screen Viewing and Talking About the Media</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n18"><dfn class="term" data-def="The practice of simultaneously using a second device (typically a smartphone or tablet) to discuss or look up content while watching television.">Second-screen viewing</dfn> typifies how media practices have changed in the digital era. Audiences no longer watch in silence: they are simultaneously tweeting reactions, looking up background information, reading other viewers&rsquo; responses, and participating in live comment threads. This transforms a traditionally passive act (watching television) into an active social practice. Media producers have responded by designing second-screen tie-ins, companion apps and live social activations around major broadcasts precisely because they know audiences are already doing this.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n20">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n21">David Gauntlett argues that audiences use media as a resource for active identity construction, drawing on their actual self and desired self; this positions audiences as agents in building who they are, not passive recipients of media values.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n22">Henry Jenkins&rsquo; concept of participatory culture (developed in <em>Textual Poachers</em>, 1992) argues that fans actively &ldquo;poach&rdquo; texts, producing fan fiction, videos and commentary &mdash; blurring the boundary between audience and producer.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n23">Parasocial relationships, the watercooler effect and second-screen viewing all demonstrate the social significance of media practices beyond simple consumption.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n19">Questions on media practices and identity often reward answers that connect Gauntlett and Jenkins to each other rather than treating them as separate points. Both argue that audiences are active &mdash; but Gauntlett focuses on identity construction through selection and identification, while Jenkins focuses on creative production and participatory remaking. If a question uses the command word &ldquo;discuss,&rdquo; consider presenting both theorists and then making a judgement about which offers more insight into the specific media form in the question.</p>

